WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:05.460
Arguably the most globally recognized mythical
creature from Australia is the bunyip, an

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.360
important staple in Aboriginal folklore.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:13.530
This amphibious being poses a threat to all
who travel near the inland waters.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:18.440
Lurking in lagoons, deep water-holes, swamps,
and rivers, this predatory creature presents

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:24.670
a clear danger to humans and animals alike,
threatening to drown or eat them at a moment’s

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:25.670
notice.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:30.150
Originating in the stories and beliefs of
the southeastern indigenous peoples of Australia,

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:35.829
accounts of the bunyip are widely varied,
but a few things are for certain: it’s big,

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:39.379
scary, and won’t hesitate to kill you.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:43.960
[Monstrum intro.]

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:49.660
The word bunyip most likely came from an Aboriginal
word for an amphibious spirit-being.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:54.010
Australian indigenous peoples believe the
beings are separate from the ancestors and

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:59.199
the Aboriginal people themselves, and were
believed to exist alongside humans.

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:01.350
The bunyip goes by several other names

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.379
depending on who is telling the story.

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:07.930
The tricky thing about bunyips is that there
are so many different accounts as to what

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.330
the creature actually looks like.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:15.690
While many of these sightings describe a four-legged
creature resembling a seal, others tell of

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:21.980
a chimera-type being resembling different
animals like emus, horses, kangaroos, and

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:22.980
alligators.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:27.560
Some stories claim the bunyip has shaggy and
long hair, while others say it has feathers

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.060
or scales.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:34.610
“Booming” noises and loud roars indicates
their presence and they are even capable of

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:37.960
causing illness.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.410
Some indigenous accounts give the bunyip the
powers of sorcery, capable of luring their

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:45.980
victims to them, which makes them even more
dangerous.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:51.130
The huge number of variations of the bunyip’s
reported appearance seem confusing at first

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.130
glance.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:56.000
But you could argue that the variation of
the creature is what has kept it alive in

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.490
Australia for so long.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:04.550
There’s no one cohesive story because there
is no one group of indigenous Aboriginal peoples.

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:10.100
Before Europeans colonized Australia there
were an estimated 250 different languages

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.600
spoken there.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.140
Then add different cultures and geographic
locations, and the variations between bunyip

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.650
accounts begin to make more sense.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:24.200
Regardless of the form this water spirit takes,
or the details of the story, the bunyip always

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.080
functions as a warning of the danger of inland
water.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.800
It serves as a lesson to children and adults
alike to respect the unpredictability of water

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.830
and the dangers it presents.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:40.500
One Aboriginal story tells of a four-legged,
milk white bunyip with a ‘big bull-head”

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.140
and large eyes that prowls the land looking
for wrongdoers to attack.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:50.280
In another story, the bunyip lurks in the
water with its glowing red eyes, attacking

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.880
a woman who goes to fetch water at night,
tearing off her arm.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:59.060
The next morning when the creature is hunted,
all that can be found is a blood-covered black

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:00.480
and red feather.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.760
In another Aboriginal story, a boy is taken

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.680
by a bunyip while playing by the river at
night.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.090
Referred to as “the old fellow, the bunyip,
takes the boy to its cave.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:14.970
An adult goes to save the boy, rubbing his
body with “dead body fat” before diving

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.880
into the river and finding the cave.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:22.880
There he waves feathers and sings, putting
the bunyip to sleep, and brings the boy back

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.450
to safety.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:29.090
Although the bunyip poses a severe threat,
harming them was often taboo.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:32.780
Another tale tells of a steamer that suddenly
comes to a halt in the river.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:37.069
The captain goes to shoot the bunyip that
is keeping the barge in place, but others

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:40.930
aboard warn him “Don’t do that, if you
do that you will die.

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:43.420
Don’t do that, you’ll die a bad death.”

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:46.690
But he doesn’t heed their warning and kills
the creature.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:52.530
The captain later gets very sick, his whole
body covered in oozing blisters, and in two

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:53.690
weeks, he is dead.

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:58.760
The stories of the bunyip were told by the
indigenous peoples with such conviction and

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:05.030
vividness that the first European explorers
and colonizers to hear of this creature initially

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:10.440
believed that it must be some prehistoric
animal that remained unidentified by European

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.880
science and believed that they would eventually
find a specimen to study.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:20.680
It’s worth keeping in mind that real Australian
fauna was totally foreign and flat-out weird

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.700
to European travelers.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:28.070
Things like black swans, wombats, platypus,
and echidnas were seen by Europeans for the

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:30.780
first time in the 19th century.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.430
Add to that the discovery of large megafauna
fossils, and the idea of the bunyip seemed

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.949
less far-fetched at the time.

00:04:37.949 --> 00:04:42.680
Take the diprotodon —an
extinct giant marsupial with claws whose appearance

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:46.030
resembles some of the large, hairy descriptions
of bunyips.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:50.449
Then there’s this slightly smaller megafauna
species [Zygomaturus trilobus] that Indigenous

00:04:50.449 --> 00:04:55.560
Australians lived alongside for at least 17,000
years.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:00.660
Newspapers in the 19th century reported bunyip
sightings, and proposed explanations.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:06.290
Theories included crocodiles, misplaced sea
mammals like seals, fossil bones, the duck-billed

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:10.560
platypus, swamp gas, and the sounds from swamp
birds.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.650
Like the Australasian bittern bird who is
commonly called the bunyip bird for its nocturnal

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:17.340
nature and booming call.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:26.460
In 1846, a book about Australia published
in London included an excerpt from an 1845

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:31.510
Australian newspaper article about the discovery
of a supposed bunyip bone, making the legend

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:32.510
international.

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:39.669
In 1847, in what is now New South Wales, a
cycloptic head was presented for examination

00:05:39.669 --> 00:05:42.330
as possible evidence of the creature.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:47.130
Advertised as a bunyip skull, it was put on
display for public viewing at the Australian

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:48.560
Museum in Sydney.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:52.130
But the specimen was eventually identified
by naturalist W.S.

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:57.350
Macleay [MAh-clay] to likely be the malformed
skull of a foal altered with taxidermy to

00:05:57.350 --> 00:05:59.710
appear even more unusual.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:04.340
While speculations of the bunyip circulated,
geographer George Windsor Earl proposed that

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:08.990
the bunyip was really a dugong, a manatee-like
water mammal.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:13.490
Some folklore says that before the rivers
were dammed in Southeastern Australia (one

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:18.570
of the bunyips most prominent areas), all
kinds of oceanic creatures from sharks to

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:22.110
small whales would occasionally travel inland.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:25.130
Australia does have the world’s largest
population of dugongs.

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:30.630
It is possible that high sea tides during
the summer and low river levels could have

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:36.259
led to an animal usually only seen at sea
making its way into more interior areas of

00:06:36.259 --> 00:06:37.259
the country.

00:06:37.259 --> 00:06:42.199
While older beliefs like the Dreaming Ancestors,
and ghosts and spirits endured, Aboriginal

00:06:42.199 --> 00:06:45.630
folklore was influenced by European cultures.

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:50.360
Which can help explain why from the mid-19th
century on, the bunyip was described more

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:56.110
as a humanoid spirit, and in one instance,
a half man half fish with a “matted crop

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:57.790
of reeds” for hair.

00:06:57.790 --> 00:07:01.919
To me it matters less what creature or thing
actually inspired the bunyip’s physical

00:07:01.919 --> 00:07:07.350
appearance than the fact that it persisted
for so long in Aboriginal culture.

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:12.419
Bunyip stories force a recognition of the
varied cultural and spiritual beliefs of the

00:07:12.419 --> 00:07:16.930
indigenous peoples of Australia before Anglo-European
intervention.

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:22.670
These tales solidify indigenous identities
and connections to the landscape in a long

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:24.479
tradition that continues today.

